%U A397300 1358325437540159513931372887098703,1734101218024805121636588450595163,1994701596274080316016872830687113
%N A397300 Brier numbers with at least three minimal covering sets.
%C A397300 Subsequence of A395816 (Brier numbers with at least two covering sets), which is itself a subsequence of A076335.
%C A397300 Let p be an odd prime and d = ord_p(2). If N*2^a == -1 (mod p), then p | N*2^k + 1 iff k == a (mod d), and, when d is even, p | N*2^k - 1 iff k == a + d/2 (mod d). Thus a finite set S of such primes, with assigned exponents a_p, certifies N as a Brier number when the classes k == a_p (mod d_p) cover the integers (so every N*2^k + 1 is divisible by some p in S) and simultaneously the classes k == a_p + d_p/2 (mod d_p) over the even-order primes of S cover the integers (so every N*2^k - 1 is likewise divisible). Any such N satisfies N == -2^(-a_p) (mod p) for all p in S and is determined modulo the product of the primes in S; the least positive odd such residue is the Brier number certified by S.
%C A397300 Covering sets are counted up to minimality: only covering sets none of whose proper subsets is itself a covering set are considered. (A covering set remains one after adjoining arbitrary primes, so without this convention every term of A076335 would have infinitely many covering sets.)
%C A397300 In the covering sets exhibited in the linked table, every prime p has ord_p(2) <= 60. This describes the certificates produced by the search; terms may admit further covering sets outside this range.
%C A397300 These are the smallest examples known - there may be smaller or intervening terms. Each listed term, however, is unconditionally a member: its three covering sets are given explicitly in the linked table. The terms were found by a covering-system search over the periods 180, 360, 720, 900, 1260, 1440, 2520 and 2700, with certificate prime products below 10^46, built on the covering {3, 5, 17, 257, 241, 97, 673} in both orientations.
%H A397300 James Shakarji, <a href="/A397300/b397300.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..3000</a>
%H A397300 James Shakarji, <a href="/A397300/a397300_2.txt">List of triplet covering sets for a(1)-a(3000)</a>
%H A397300 James Shakarji, <a href="/A397300/a397300_1.py.txt">Sierpinski/Riesel-residue propagation with minimum-weight covers and CRT descent for the generation of Brier numbers with at least three covering sets</a>
%e A397300 For k = a(1) = 70697336030631707468518603979107 and every n >= 1, both k*2^n+1 and k*2^n-1 have a divisor in each of the three sets
%e A397300   {3,5,7,11,13,17,19,31,37,41,61,73,97,109,151,241,257,673},
%e A397300   {3,5,7,11,13,17,19,31,37,41,73,97,109,151,241,257,331,673},
%e A397300   {3,5,7,11,13,17,19,31,37,41,73,97,109,151,241,257,673,1321},
%e A397300 so a(1) has three covering sets and is a term.
